|                        | **In-house**                          | **Outsource**                                 |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| **Meaning**            | Work is done by your own company/team | Work is given to an outside company or person |
| **Who does the work?** | Your employees                        | External company, freelancer, or agency       |
| **Example**            | Your company has its own AI developer | You hire an AI freelancer                     |
| **Control**            | More control                          | Less direct control                           |
| **Cost**               | Salary, office, equipment, etc.       | Usually pay per project/service               |
| **Best for**           | Regular, long-term work               | Specialized or temporary work                 |


============

Simple example: Your Business Consultant AI 🤖
In-house

Imagine you own a company and employ:

AI Developer
Python Developer
Business Analyst

They work inside your company.

➡️ This is In-house development.

Outsource

Your company does not have an AI developer.

So you give the project to:

Sugumar AI Services — Build a Business Consultant AI for ₹X

You are an external person/company doing the work for them.

➡️ This is Outsourcing.

Very simple formula

In-house = We do it ourselves 🏢

Outsource = We give the work to someone outside 🤝

Real-life example 🍰

A bakery needs a website.

In-house:
The bakery has its own IT employee who builds the website.

Outsource:
The bakery hires a web developer or IT company outside to build it.

So if a company hires you as a freelancer to create an AI chatbot, AI agent, website, or automation, you are doing outsourced work for that company.

